Looking at this function, I can identify **two bugs**:

## Bug 1: Wrong comparison (line 9)

```rust
if data[i] == max_val {
```

This compares the current element against the *best value found so far* (`max_val`), not against the *previous element*. To detect a continuing streak, you need to compare `data[i]` with `data[i - 1]`. As written, once `max_val` is set to `2`, the `1`s are never seen as a continuing streak because they don't equal `2`.

## Bug 2: Final streak never checked (missing code after loop)

If the longest streak is at the **end** of the array, `current_count` is never compared against `max_count` after the loop finishes. In this case, the `1,1,1` streak (length 3) is the last one, so it never gets promoted to `max_count`.

## Tracing the original code with `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`:

| i | data[i] | comparison | current_count | max_count | max_val |
|---|---------|-----------|--------------|-----------|---------|
| – | –       | init      | 1            | 1         | 2       |
| 1 | 2       | 2==2 ✓   | 2            | 1         | 2       |
| 2 | 1       | 1≠2, 2>1 | 1            | 2         | 2       |
| 3 | 1       | 1≠2, 1≤2 | 1            | 2         | 2       |
| 4 | 1       | 1≠2, 1≤2 | 1            | 2         | 2       |

Result: `(2, 2)` — wrong. The `1`s never register as a growing streak, and the final streak is never checked.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else if current_count > max_count {
            max_count = current_count;
            max_val = data[i - 1];
            current_count = 1;
        } else {
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

